WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
A mouse reels from side to side as it walks.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.440
Its cerebellum is small.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.920
The layers of its cerebral cortex are disorganized, and roughly in reverse order.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.160
Why would a mouse that cannot walk straight have a cortex built in the wrong order?

5
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.920
One missing protein explains both.

6
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:31.383
Neurons leave the ventricular zone and climb toward the surface.

7
00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:35.363
The first ones form a thin sheet just under it.

8
00:00:35.923 --> 00:00:39.803
The next ones move into the middle of that sheet and split it in two.

9
00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:44.843
The upper part, the marginal zone, holds Cajal-Retzius cells.

10
00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:51.623
Each new wave climbs past the last, so early-born neurons end up deep and late-

11
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:53.303
born neurons end up superficial.

12
00:00:54.233 --> 00:01:00.373
Was reeler making the wrong neurons, or putting the right ones in the wrong

13
00:01:01.753 --> 00:01:06.893
place? Birthdating showed the same classes of neurons, born on the same schedule as in

14
00:01:06.893 --> 00:01:13.073
normal mice. But early-born neurons tended to sit near the surface, and late-born

15
00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:19.633
neurons deep. The first sheet fails to split, and each later wave tends to stop

16
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:21.553
beneath the neurons already there.

17
00:01:21.553 --> 00:01:26.293
The neurons are made, but they cannot get past their elders.

18
00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:32.406
Cajal-Retzius cells make a very large secreted protein called Reelin.

19
00:01:33.106 --> 00:01:38.306
It is made at the top of the cortex, the very place where each new wave should stop.

20
00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:45.366
Reelin binds two lipoprotein receptors, ApoER2 and VLDLR.

21
00:01:46.386 --> 00:01:51.906
Inside the neuron, the kinases Fyn and Src then add a phosphate to an adaptor

22
00:01:51.906 --> 00:01:53.206
called Dab1.

23
00:01:54.016 --> 00:01:59.116
In a relay race, remove any runner and the baton never arrives.

24
00:02:00.356 --> 00:02:02.116
Reeler mice lack Reelin.

25
00:02:03.096 --> 00:02:05.396
Mice without Dab1 look the same.

26
00:02:06.236 --> 00:02:08.176
So do mice without both receptors.

27
00:02:09.436 --> 00:02:15.756
Matching mutants give no order, but biochemistry did: brains without Reelin held

28
00:02:15.756 --> 00:02:19.376
more Dab1 and less phosphorylated Dab1.

29
00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.430
Reelin is needed. But what does it tell a neuron?

30
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:31.050
Perhaps stop here, or let go of the glial fiber, or come toward me.

31
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.750
The experiments point different ways, so the question is open.

32
00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:40.810
A missing signal shows only that it is needed, not what it says.
